//? Title: Inverse Iteration: One Factorization, Actual Eigenvector Histories
//? Subtitle: Fixed shifts, rounded spectra, starting components and residual controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A fixed shift turns a nearby eigenvector into the dominant direction of a linear
solve. Factor the shifted matrix once, solve repeatedly and normalize each
actual iterate. Starting with a two-dimensional calculation, we preserve the
original rounded three-dimensional matrix and distinguish its actual spectrum
from its intended spectrum. Actual residual histories expose convergence,
floating-point plateaus, missing starting components and equal-distance
failures. No dataset is needed for these controlled linear-algebra experiments.
)
\toc

> Start with two components

Take A=diag(2,5), shift 2.1 and the normalized equal-entry start. The shifted
matrix is diag(−0.1,2.9); solving divides the two coordinates by these entries.
The first unnormalized direction is approximately (−7.0711,0.2438), and
normalization changes its length without changing its direction. The unwanted to
wanted component ratio is multiplied by −1/29 each step. Eigenvector sign can
alternate, so a sign change is not a convergence failure. The diagram shows the
actual first transition from the single producing run.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/inverse_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/inverse_iteration_analysis.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t =
        dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_trace({{2, 0}, {0, 5}}, 2.1, {3});
    std::print("| solve | x0 | x1 | Rayleigh | residual |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &s : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8e} |\n", s.iteration,
                   s.pair.vector[0], s.pair.vector[1], s.pair.value, s.residual);
    std::print("Factorizations {}, inverse solves {}.\n", t.run.factorizations,
               t.run.solves);
    dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_diagram(t).save_svg(
        "build/inverse_tiny.svg");
}
```
