> Derive convergence and identify the assumptions

For a real symmetric matrix with orthonormal eigenvectors, expand the start in
that basis. A nonsingular fixed shift gives the unnormalized direction

$$
z_k=(A-\mu I)^{-k}x_0=\sum_i c_i(\lambda_i-\mu)^{-k}v_i.
$$
\alttext(Each initial eigenvector component is repeatedly divided by its shifted eigenvalue.)

Normalization cancels in ratios. If j is a simple, uniquely nearest eigenvalue
and its starting coefficient is nonzero, every unwanted ratio satisfies

$$
\frac{z_{k,i}}{z_{k,j}}=\frac{c_i}{c_j}
\left(\frac{\lambda_j-\mu}{\lambda_i-\mu}\right)^k,
\qquad
\rho=\max_{i\ne j}\left|\frac{\lambda_j-\mu}{\lambda_i-\mu}\right|<1.
$$
\alttext(Unwanted to wanted component ratios contract geometrically at most by rho per inverse solve.)

This gives geometric directional convergence in exact arithmetic. A zero wanted
coefficient remains zero; a tied nearest distance gives no strict contraction.
For repeated nearest eigenvalues the limit may be any direction in their
eigenspace. General nonsymmetric inputs are accepted by the implementation, but
this orthonormal-basis argument does not establish a guarantee for them.

The reported scalar is the Rayleigh quotient, not the fixed shift. For a unit
iterate, orthogonality gives

$$
\widehat\lambda=\frac{x^TAx}{x^Tx},\qquad
\widehat\lambda-\lambda_j=\sum_{i\ne j}(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)(v_i^Tx)^2.
$$
\alttext(The Rayleigh eigenvalue error is quadratic in the unwanted normalized eigenvector components.)

Thus eigenvalue error can be much smaller than directional error. The absolute
residual is the norm of A*x−value*x. The dimensionless displayed diagnostic uses
the Frobenius norm:

$$
\eta=\frac{\lVert Ax-\widehat\lambda x\rVert_2}
{(\lVert A\rVert_F+|\widehat\lambda|)\lVert x\rVert_2}.
$$
\alttext(The relative residual scales the absolute residual by matrix and eigenvalue magnitudes and the vector norm.)

For symmetric A, the distance from the Rayleigh value to some eigenvalue is at
most residual/norm(x): expand the residual in the eigenbasis and bound every
squared shifted eigenvalue below by the nearest squared distance. This does not
identify the intended eigenvector or prove the input matrix matches its design.

> Preserve the original rounded matrix

The original experiment forms Q*diag(2,5,9)*Q-transpose with rows of Q equal to
(0.6667,−0.6667,0.3333), (0.6667,0.3333,−0.6667), and (0.3333,0.6667,0.6667).
These rounded columns are not exactly orthonormal. Consequently a tiny residual
at about 5.000333 is a correct solution of the supplied matrix, not exact
recovery of the intended eigenvalue five. The next program retains all original
entries, shift 5.05 and sixty solves. Every plotted residual is captured during
that one run, including the start; it does not rerun the solver with increasing
iteration caps.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/inverse_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/inverse_iteration_analysis.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto a = dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_fixture();
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_trace(a, 5.05, {60});
    std::print("| input | final Rayleigh | difference from intended five | absolute residual | scaled " "residual |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::print("| original rounded Q | {:.12f} | {:.8e} | {:.8e} | {:.8e} |\n",
               t.run.pair.value, t.run.pair.value - 5, t.run.residual,
               t.run.relative_residual);
    std::print("Final vector ({:.8f}, {:.8f}, {:.8f}).\n", t.run.pair.vector[0],
               t.run.pair.vector[1], t.run.pair.vector[2]);
    std::print("Stored states {}, factorizations {}, inverse solves {}.\n",
               t.steps.size(), t.run.factorizations, t.run.solves);
    dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_residual_plot({t}, {"original rounded Q"}).title("Original matrix: actual residuals and a numerical plateau").ylim(-19, 4).save_svg("build/inverse_original.svg");
}
```
