> Control the intended spectrum and vary only the shift

For the control, use rows (2,−2,1)/3, (2,1,−2)/3 and (1,2,2)/3. Their columns
are orthonormal in exact arithmetic, with spectrum 2,5,9 for the constructed
matrix. Floating arithmetic still rounds the entries and products. All three
shifts below have five as their uniquely nearest eigenvalue. Their predicted
directional contraction factors are 0.2 for shift 4.5, about 0.0163934 for 5.05,
and 0.6 for 6.5. Each curve is a separate eight-solve experiment using the same
matrix and start. A residual is not itself the angle, so its finite-step ratios
need not equal these asymptotic directional factors.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/inverse_shifts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/inverse_iteration_analysis.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto a = dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_fixture(false);
    std::vector<dm::algorithms::InverseIterationTrace> traces;
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    std::print("| fixed shift | Rayleigh after eight solves | absolute residual | scaled residual |\n| ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (double mu : {4.5, 5.05, 6.5}) {
        traces.push_back(dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_trace(a, mu, {8}));
        labels.push_back(std::format("shift {}", mu));
        const auto &r = traces.back().run;
        std::print("| {:.2f} | {:.12f} | {:.8e} | {:.8e} |\n", mu, r.pair.value,
                   r.residual, r.relative_residual);
    }
    dm::algorithms::inverse_iteration_residual_plot(traces, labels).title(
        "Same matrix and start, three fixed shifts").ylim(-19, 4).save_svg(
        "build/inverse_shifts.svg");
}
```
