// result_end
\caption(Zero residual alone does not select the intended eigenvalue. Zero residual is displayed at the declared 1e-18 floor.)
\alttext(One convergent residual curve, one zero-residual wrong-target curve at the display floor, and one constant tied-distance residual.)

> Numerical policy, costs and ownership

The fixed shift is factored once by dense partial-pivot LU. Every actual solve
uses the saved row swaps and triangular factors; Rayleigh diagnostics never
update the shift. Costs are O(n cubed + k*n squared) arithmetic and O(n squared)
working storage, with O(k*n) additional stored vector entries in a trace. Unlike
the old repeated elimination, factorization is not repeated for each solve. This
is a dense implementation, not a sparse eigensolver.

The kernel validates a nonempty finite square matrix and a finite shift. An
omitted start uses equal positive entries; a supplied start must have the
matching dimension, finite entries and a nonzero norm. Normalization uses a
robust hypot norm and extended-precision arithmetic, while stored vector entries
and diagnostics are doubles. Nonfinite arithmetic or diagnostics outside the
double range throw. This improves range behavior but does not guarantee forward
accuracy for an arbitrarily ill-conditioned problem. Extended precision is
platform dependent, so final digits and plateau values can differ.

For symmetric A, the shifted condition number is the largest absolute shifted
eigenvalue divided by the smallest. Moving the shift nearer the wanted
eigenvalue improves the exact directional contraction while making the solve
more ill-conditioned. A small residual must be measured; ill-conditioning alone
does not prove either success or failure. We do not form an explicit inverse.
The
[Netlib inverse-iteration reference|https://netlib.org/utk/people/JackDongarra/etemplates/node96.html]
gives the nonorthogonal-start assumption, contraction factor and linear-solve
formulation.

Runs perform exactly the requested number of solves with no residual-based
stopping. Zero solves returns the normalized start and its diagnostics without
factoring, even for a singular shift. Negative legacy counts now throw. The
original function signature and EigenPair layout remain available, but floating
results may change from the old double elimination and exact singular pivots now
throw rather than silently substituting a tiny denominator. The
Rayleigh-iteration compatibility helpers retain their previous numerical policy;
these fixed-shift guarantees do not apply to that separate algorithm.

Run/trace options default to fifty solves, matrix order at most 1024 and at most
one million retained vector entries. Trace admission checks the complete n*(k+1)
entries, including the start, before numerical work. A zero optional cap
disables it; arithmetic/container limits still apply. Untraced runs ignore the
history cap. The legacy convenience call disables the optional order/history
caps. Borrowed input and start are never changed or retained. Returned vectors
and histories are owned, with no shared mutable state or prefix replay. The
history ceiling excludes scalar records, the final duplicate result vector,
working LU, borrowed input, vector capacity and byte/peak RAM; it is not a
physical memory quota. Graphics apply a separate complete point quota and
validate displayed scalar history fields, not edited-history provenance.

Every figure uses log10(max(actual residual,1e-18)) where applicable. A floor
point says only that the computed residual was at or below the display floor; it
does not establish an additional eighteen digits of accuracy. The original
report's purported log-scale figure lacked a logarithmic transformation; this
replacement transforms the plotted coordinate explicitly.

> Verification and scope

Nine selected native cases pass AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer,
including every diagonal iterate, 400 known rotated-spectrum paths, the original
rounded matrix, pivoting, extreme scales, ownership, admission and graphics. The
existing 300 random symmetric nearest-eigenpair checks also pass within that
selected suite. An independent 90-digit Decimal Gauss-Jordan oracle checks 316
inputs, all 4388 actual states and 4072 inverse solves, recomputing every next
direction, Rayleigh value and both residual diagnostics. The maximum next-vector
discrepancy is below 5.6e-17. NumPy independently confirms the rounded matrix's
middle eigenvalue 5.000333314809198 and the exact-basis control. On 402
nonsingular controls the frozen legacy implementation and new kernel agree
within 2.7e-15 in eigenvalue and 2.3e-16 in sign-aligned direction.

All forty module/helper definitions are documented, ten public examples compile
and run, and four representative clangd hovers pass. This is a fixed-shift
audit. The separate existing Rayleigh-iteration convergence test fails with the
frozen original header too; its helper bodies are unchanged here. No claim is
made that that separate solver or the entire historical report corpus passes
this audit.
